          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Log Splitter Do You Need for Your Firewood Volume?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the machine to how many cords you split each year; that single number matters more than tons, horsepower or price. A cord is the standard US firewood measure: a stack 4 feet high, 4 feet deep and 8 feet long, or 128 cubic feet. A household that burns a face cord or two in a backyard fire pit needs a completely different splitter from one heating a house through the winter on six to eight full cords.
          </p>

          <p>
            Below one cord a year, a small electric or manual splitter, or simply a good maul, does the job. Between one and three cords, a quality electric can keep up if the wood is seasoned and of moderate size, though a mid-size gas splitter saves real time. Past three cords, particularly with big hardwood rounds, a 20 to 27-ton gas splitter with vertical operation is the right tool, and owners at the top of that range tend to prize speed and auto-return above everything else.
          </p>

