          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does the kind of wood change the size you need?</h3>

          <p>
            Yes. Straight-grained, seasoned oak, ash and maple split far more readily than green elm, sweetgum or knotty crotch wood. If you drop and buck your own trees with a <a href="/best-chainsaw/">chainsaw</a>, you will end up with a mix, including stubborn pieces from near the base and from the branches. Size the splitter for those hard pieces, not for the easy ones.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Should your back factor into the choice?</h3>

          <p>
            It should. Hoisting 60 to 100-pound rounds onto a waist-high beam for hours is punishing work. If big rounds are part of your pile, vertical operation stops being a luxury, because it lets you roll each round onto the foot plate rather than lift it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much storage space does a log splitter take?</h3>

          <p>
            A towable gas splitter occupies roughly the footprint of a small trailer, needs a level spot to work on, and wants a dry place to sit for the rest of the year. An electric or manual unit fits on a garage shelf or in a corner. On a small lot with a tight garage, that alone can settle the matter in favor of electric, as long as your wood is seasoned and of moderate size.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tonnage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Tons Does a Log Splitter Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For seasoned, straight-grained rounds of modest diameter, 4 to 7 tons from a small electric is enough; for nearly all homeowner firewood, including bigger and knottier rounds, plan on a 20 to 27-ton gas splitter. Commercial machines rated above that range are built for production firewood and very large, stringy wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tonnage figure is the most force the hydraulic ram can apply, worked out from the cylinder size and the pump's peak pressure. It tells you whether a machine can push through a tough round, but past a certain point extra tons do not split ordinary firewood any faster. Brands also calculate and advertise the rating in different ways, so a 25-ton model from one maker is not necessarily weaker than a 27-ton model from another.
          </p>

