          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does the condition of the wood change the force needed?</h3>

          <p>
            A great deal. Seasoned wood that already shows checking cracks at the ends gives way far more easily than freshly cut green wood. Some species, elm and sweetgum above all, have interlocked grain that fights the wedge whatever the tonnage, so they stay slow even on large machines. Knots and crotches also demand much more force than clean, straight sections.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Is more tonnage always better?</h3>

          <p>
            No. Buy enough force for the toughest wood you split on a regular basis, then put the rest of the budget into speed, vertical operation and build quality. A 22-ton splitter with a fast cycle will usually clear a pile sooner than a 30-ton splitter with a slow one.
          </p>
