        <div id="cycle-time" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is a Good Cycle Time for a Log Splitter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many gas splitters list a cycle time somewhere between 10 and 20 seconds, and small electric splitters are usually slower; for a big pile, faster is better. Cycle time is how long the ram needs to extend fully and come back. Across hundreds of splits, a few seconds per stroke turns into hours. A quicker cycle also means less standing around with idle hands, and so less temptation to reach in while the ram is still moving.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does a two-stage pump matter?</h3>

          <p>
            It is what makes gas splitters cycle quickly. The pump drives the ram fast at low pressure until it meets the wood, then switches to slow, high-pressure travel to do the splitting. Once the log pops apart, it shifts back to high speed. Treat a two-stage pump as a requirement on any gas splitter you shortlist.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does an auto-return valve do?</h3>

          <p>
            It saves time on every stroke. After the split, you push the lever into the return position and let go; a detent holds it there and releases it back to neutral once the ram is fully retracted, leaving your hands free to set up the next piece. Without one, you have to hold the lever for the entire return stroke.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">SPEED VS SAFETY</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A quick cycle is never a reason to hurry.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Do not position or nudge a log while the ram is moving, even on a slow machine. Let the ram stop or retract completely, then reposition. The speed should come from the splitter, not from shortcuts with your hands.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can you compare cycle times between brands?</h3>

          <p>
            Only loosely, because brands measure under different conditions. Use the listed figures to tell fast machines from slow ones within the same class, and weigh them alongside tonnage and features rather than on their own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gas, Electric or Manual: Which Type of Log Splitter Should You Buy?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose gas if you split more than a cord or two a year or your wood includes large rounds; choose electric if you split a small amount of seasoned wood and want quiet and simplicity. For most homeowners the real decision is between those two, with manual and kinetic splitters serving narrower needs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas hydraulic splitters</h3>

          <p>
            These are the standard for serious firewood. A small gas engine drives a hydraulic pump, force usually starts at 20 tons, many convert to vertical operation, and they tow behind a vehicle. The downsides are noise, engine maintenance and a size that overwhelms many small properties, but they handle volume and difficult wood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric hydraulic splitters</h3>

          <p>
            Running on household current, they are quiet, clean and low-maintenance, so you can work close to the house or in an open garage. Expect roughly 4 to 10 tons, horizontal operation only, and slower cycling than gas. They suit fireplace and fire pit volumes of seasoned wood. Use the extension cord gauge and length the manual calls for, or plug straight into a GFCI outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual and kinetic splitters</h3>

          <p>
            A manual hydraulic splitter uses a hand-pumped jack to drive the ram. It needs no power and is quiet and cheap, but every split takes many strokes, so it only makes sense for small amounts. A kinetic splitter stores energy in a heavy spinning flywheel and drives the ram very quickly; it is fast, generally pricier, and calls for extra caution precisely because the ram moves so fast.
          </p>

