        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Log Splitters
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Tonnage Instead of Speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Nearly all homeowner firewood splits within the reach of a 20 to 27-ton gas machine, and tons beyond that seldom speed anything up. How long your pile takes depends on cycle time, a two-stage pump and auto-return. Buy enough force for your hardest regular wood, then spend on speed and features.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Two-Person Operation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                One person loading while another works the lever is a textbook way to crush fingers, since the operator cannot see or sense exactly where the loader's hands are. Manufacturers warn against the practice. The same person should place the log and run the control, with helpers standing well away from the machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Lifting Heavy Rounds Onto the Beam</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hoisting big rounds to waist height over and over strains backs and ends with logs dropped on feet. If your pile includes large rounds, choose a splitter with vertical operation and roll the heavy pieces onto the foot plate instead. A hookaroon moves rounds into place without bending.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Gas Splitter in a Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas engines emit carbon monoxide, an invisible, odorless gas that can reach dangerous levels fast in an enclosed space, even with the door open. Run gas splitters only outdoors, away from doors and windows. If you need to split right beside the house, an electric model is the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Feeling for Hydraulic Leaks by Hand</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fluid from a pinhole leak in a pressurized line can be forced through skin, an injury that needs emergency treatment even when it looks minor. Test a suspected leak with cardboard, relieve pressure before loosening any fitting, and swap out damaged hoses instead of patching them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many tons of splitting force do I actually need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For seasoned, straight-grained firewood of moderate diameter, a 4 to 7-ton electric splitter does the job. For most homeowner firewood, bigger and knottier rounds included, a 20 to 27-ton gas splitter is the practical range. Very large, green or stringy wood such as elm favors the upper end. Past that point, speed and features count for more than extra tons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is an electric splitter good enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for fireplace and fire pit volumes of seasoned wood. Electric splitters are quiet, clean and need little maintenance, and you can use them near the house. They cycle slower than gas, usually work horizontally only, and can stall on large, knotty or green wood. If you split more than a cord or two a year, or your rounds are big, gas is the better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I rent a splitter instead of buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rent if you split once a year and handle a cord or two. Have the wood cut and staged before you collect the machine so you get the most from the rental period. If you split every year, or several cords at a time, buying usually pays for itself within a few seasons, especially if you share the machine with neighbors.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cycle time should I look for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many gas splitters list cycle times somewhere in the 10 to 20-second range, and quicker is better for big piles. Small electric splitters are generally slower. A two-stage pump and an auto-return valve both make a noticeable difference to speed. Because brands measure under different conditions, use cycle times to compare machines within one class rather than across brands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is vertical splitting worth paying extra for?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you split large, heavy rounds. Vertical operation lets you roll a round onto the foot plate instead of lifting it onto a waist-high beam, which protects your back and makes very heavy pieces manageable. If your wood is all moderate pieces that are easy to lift, a horizontal-only machine is perfectly fine for the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should firewood season after splitting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most hardwoods need at least six months, and often a year or more, stacked off the ground in a sunny, breezy spot with the top covered and the sides open. The EPA's Burn Wise program recommends a moisture content of 20 percent or less for clean burning, and an inexpensive moisture meter tells you when the wood has reached it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can two people run a log splitter together?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers warn against it. When one person loads and another works the control, the operator cannot reliably see where the loader's hands are, and crushing injuries follow. The safe setup is a single operator who both positions the log and runs the control, with any helpers stacking splits well clear of the machine while it runs.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

